            "topic": "Probability",
            "title": "Combinations",
            "question": "A quality control engineer must randomly select exactly 4 concrete cylinders for testing from a massive batch of 20 available cylinders. Because the specific order of selection is entirely irrelevant, this is a Combination. The number of mathematically distinct ways to choose these 4 cylinders is:",
            "question_image": "",
            "times_presented": 0,
            "options": [
                {
                    "label": "A",
                    "text": "$20! / 4!$",
                    "is_correct": false
                },
                {
                    "label": "B",
                    "text": "$20^4$",
                    "is_correct": false
                },
                {
                    "label": "C",
                    "text": "$20! / (16! \\cdot 4!) = 4845$",
                    "is_correct": true
                },
                {
                    "label": "D",
                    "text": "$20 \\times 19 \\times 18 \\times 17 = 116,280$",
                    "is_correct": false
                }
            ],
            "solution": {
                "steps": [
                    {
                        "title": "Permutation vs Combination",
                        "content": "If order mattered (e.g., 1st cylinder gets destroyed, 2nd gets kept), it would be a permutation ($116,280$). Because order does not matter, picking (A,B,C,D) is exactly the same mathematical event as picking (D,C,B,A)."
                    },
                    {
                        "title": "The Formula",
                        "content": "The combination formula $C(n,r) = n! / ((n-r)! \\cdot r!)$. The extra $r!$ in the denominator specifically divides out and removes all the duplicate, out-of-order copies."
                    }
                ],
                "solution_image": "",
                "video_explanation": "",
                "final_answer": "C"
            },
