            "topic": "Water distribution systems",
            "title": "Equivalent Pipes (Series)",
            "question": "Two pipes are connected in series in a water distribution network. Pipe 1 has a length of 1,000 ft, diameter of 8 inches, and Hazen-Williams C = 100. Pipe 2 has a length of 500 ft, diameter of 6 inches, and C = 100. Which of the following principles applies to this configuration?",
            "question_image": "",
            "times_presented": 0,
            "options": [
                {
                    "label": "A",
                    "text": "The head loss across each pipe is identical.",
                    "is_correct": false
                },
                {
                    "label": "B",
                    "text": "The flow rate through both pipes is the same.",
                    "is_correct": true
                },
                {
                    "label": "C",
                    "text": "The total head loss is the average of the individual head losses.",
                    "is_correct": false
                },
                {
                    "label": "D",
                    "text": "The equivalent diameter is the sum of the individual diameters.",
                    "is_correct": false
                }
            ],
            "solution": {
                "steps": [
                    {
                        "title": "Analyze Series Connection",
                        "content": "When pipes are connected end-to-end (in series), water has only one path to follow."
                    },
                    {
                        "title": "Apply Continuity",
                        "content": "According to the principle of continuity, the flow rate ($Q$) entering the first pipe must equal the flow rate exiting the second pipe, meaning $Q_{total} = Q_1 = Q_2$."
                    },
                    {
                        "title": "Analyze Head Loss",
                        "content": "The total head loss is the sum of the head losses through each pipe: $h_{total} = h_1 + h_2$. Therefore, the flow rate is the same, not the head loss."
                    }
                ],
                "final_answer": "B",
                "solution_image": "",
                "video_explanation": ""
            },
